*State Department Human Rights Report*

Section 3. Freedom to Participate in the Political Process

The constitution and law provide citizens the ability to choose their government in free and fair periodic elections held by secret ballot and based on universal and equal suffrage.

Elections and Political Participation

Recent Elections : The president won a second five-year term. Voters also elected new members of the parliament, as well as members of the regional legislatures. [POPULATION FIGURE] national political parties contested the legislative elections, and [POPULATION FIGURE] secured seats in the parliament by passing the 4 percent parliamentary threshold. Domestic and international observers deemed the elections to be free and fair.
In recent months, however, thousands of supporters of the losing presidential candidate protested the president's victory. The demonstrations turned violent, resulting in more than 700 injuries and 10 deaths. The security forces denied their officers were involved in any of the deaths. A video uploaded by protesters that circulated widely online showed a man being kicked and beaten by 10 personnel from the security forces' paramilitary unit. Subsequently, these personnel received 21 days of detention for their involvement in the incident. The security forces began an investigation, but as of recent months had not released any results.
Political Parties and Political Participation : By law parties require 4 percent of the vote to qualify to win seats in the legislature. The law also stipulates that to nominate a presidential candidate, a party or coalition of parties must have received 25 percent of the national vote or won 20 percent of the seats in the legislature in the previous national election.
All adult citizens age 17 or older are eligible to vote, except members of the security forces, convicts serving a sentence of five years or more, persons with mental disabilities, and persons deprived of voting rights by an irrevocable court verdict. Married individuals younger than age 17 are considered legal adults and eligible to vote.
The law stipulates that only persons with an official identification card are permitted to be on the voter list. Despite a regulation allowing citizens to use an official letter from the civil registration office as a substitute, there were reports that some voters were not able to vote in the elections, including members of certain ethnic minority communities who did not have an official identification card. There were also accusations that inaccuracies in the voter list disenfranchised some voters.
Participation of Women and Minorities : No law limits participation of women and members of minorities in the political process, and they participated. The law on political parties mandates that women comprise a minimum of 30 percent of the founding membership of a new political party.
The law requires parties to nominate women for 30 percent of the candidate slots on their party lists. In recent elections, [POPULATION FIGURE] out of [POPULATION FIGURE] (approximately 40 percent) of parliamentary candidates were women, competing for [POPULATION FIGURE] seats. The election commission reported that [POPULATION FIGURE] women (20.5 percent) were elected to the parliament, up from [POPULATION FIGURE] in previous years.
In the regional legislatures, to which each region elects representatives, [POPULATION FIGURE] of [POPULATION FIGURE] members elected were women (30.9 percent). A female governor was elected in a major region.
There were no official statistics on the ethnic backgrounds of legislators in the parliament. [POPULATION FIGURE] of the [POPULATION FIGURE] members of the president's new cabinet were non-members of the majority religious group and [POPULATION FIGURE] were women, a decrease from the previous cabinet which included [POPULATION FIGURE] women.

