*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]

## Overview

[COUNTRY] has made impressive democratic gains since the fall of an authoritarian regime, establishing significant pluralism in politics and the media and undergoing multiple, peaceful transfers of power between parties. However, the country continues to struggle with challenges including systemic corruption, discrimination and violence against minority groups, separatist tensions in a region with a distinct ethnic identity, and the politicized use of defamation and blasphemy laws.

## Key Developments

The president of the [RULING PARTY] was reelected, defeating a former general in a rematch of their previous contest. The former general's supporters held a major protest in the capital to object to the president's reelection. The protest turned violent, with rioters using projectiles and setting fires, and police using tear gas and water cannons; at least 6 people were killed during the clashes. 
Student groups held nationwide protests against proposed revisions to the criminal code that would have restricted extramarital sex and access to contraceptives for minors, as well as legislation that weakened the country's anticorruption agency. Protesters and security forces again clashed; two students were killed by police, and hundreds of people were injured.
Authorities continued a crackdown on proindependence activity in a region with a distinct ethnic identity throughout the year. Ten protesters from a certain ethnic minority community and one member of the security forces died in clashes that took place after police were filmed using racial slurs while arresting a group of students from the same ethnic minority; later that year, at least 110 people were arrested for flying a flag considered treasonous by the authorities.

---

## A Electoral Process

The president is directly elected and serves as both head of state and head of government. Presidents and vice presidents can serve up to two five-year terms. The president won a second term in a recent election with 55.5 percent of the vote, defeating a former general, the [OPPOSITION PARTY] candidate.
Limited voting irregularities were reported, but the contest was largely considered free and fair by international election monitors. The [OPPOSITION PARTY] campaign claimed the election was marred by widespread fraud and vote rigging, but this claim was rejected by the supreme court. Later, the president appointed the former general as the defense minister.
The parliament, the main legislative chamber, consists of 575 members elected in 34 multimember districts. The 136-member upper house is responsible for monitoring laws related to regional autonomy and may also propose bills on the topic. All legislators serve five-year terms with no term limits.
Legislative elections were held concurrently with the presidential race and local contests. The [RULING PARTY], led by a former president, won 19.3 percent of the vote and 128 seats. A party associated with a former authoritarian leader won 85 seats with 12.3 percent of the vote, followed by the [OPPOSITION PARTY] with 78 seats and 12.6 percent of the vote. Another party won 59 seats, while a party of a former president won 54. Two Islamic parties lost seats from the last parliament, returning with 44 and 19 respectively. Two other Islamic parties increased their representation, winning 58 and 50 seats respectively.
The legal framework for elections is largely democratic, and electoral authorities are mostly seen as impartial. However, some legal provisions are problematic. Under legislation passed that year, a hereditary leader of a region is that region’s unelected governor.
A revision to the law governing local elections requires that the election oversight body and the electoral commission conduct a binding consultation with the parliament and the government before issuing any new regulations or decisions. Activists expressed concerns that the rules would reduce electoral authorities’ independence.
In recent years, the parliament approved a new electoral law that requires parties or coalitions fielding presidential candidates to hold 20 percent of the seats in parliament or 25 percent of the national vote. The provision effectively bars new or smaller parties from fielding candidates in the presidential race.

---

## B Political Pluralism and Participation

The right to organize political parties is respected, and the system features competition among several major parties. Four new parties contested the elections that year, two of them led by children of the former president.
However, election laws favor large parties by increasing eligibility requirements. Legislation passed in recent years requires new parties to undergo a “factual verification” process which involves confirming the accuracy of submitted documents on parties’ management, membership, and operations.
Certain ideologies are banned, and those who disseminate related symbols or promote these ideologies can face prison sentences of up to 12 years. In recent years, police arrested members of a literary group in a major city for possessing books on a banned ideology, including the biography of a former leader of a [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP]. Later that year, police in the capital of a province seized books on a related ideology during a raid.
[COUNTRY] has established a pattern of democratic power transfers between rival parties for many years. The most recent handover occurred several years ago, when [RULING PARTY] returned to power after losing the previous two presidential elections. However, legislation passed in recent years makes it more difficult for the opposition by tying presidential candidates’ eligibility to their parties’ past electoral success.
While voters and candidates are generally free from undue interference, the security forces remain influential, with former commanders playing prominent roles in politics, and intimidation by nonstate actors—including a jihadist militant group—remains a problem.
Women enjoy full political rights, and political parties are also subject to 30 percent gender quotas for steering committees and candidates. Women remain underrepresented in electoral politics, holding 20.5 percent of the parliament’s seats; however, women do win leadership positions. Recently, the parliament elected a woman to serve as its first female speaker.
A certain ethnic minority group is poorly represented in politics, and often abstain from voting. However, two parties with leaders from this ethnic group contested the elections that year. Both parties fell below the 4-percent threshold to earn seats.
A religious minority group, who are also poorly represented in electoral politics, abstain from voting.
Some local governments have discriminated against [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] by restricting access to identification cards, birth certificates, marriage licenses, and other bureaucratic necessities, limiting their political rights and electoral opportunities.

