*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]

## Status Change

[COUNTRY]’s status declined from Free to Partly Free because criminal groups continue to commit acts of violence and intimidation against politicians, ordinary citizens, and religious congregants, and because the justice system has been hampered by obstruction and politicization.

## Overview

Elections in [COUNTRY] are largely credible and free. However, corruption is a serious problem that undermines democracy and rule of law, and violence remains a grave problem. Authorities have pursued a harsh, militarized response to the country’s gangs, resulting in allegations of abuse. The country has a lively press and civil society sector, though journalists risk harassment and violence in connection with work related to gang activity or corruption.

## Key Developments

The president, the [RULING PARTY] candidate, was elected in the first round of voting, easily defeating the long-dominant [OPPOSITION PARTY] and another major party. The president became the first third-party candidate to win the presidency since the end of the conflict for many years.
In recent years, the president stopped asylum seekers aiming to present their cases at the border of another country from traveling through [COUNTRY], despite warnings from [NGO] that [COUNTRY]’s pervasive violence made it an unsuitable third country for asylum seekers.
Forced disappearances rose by over 20 percent in the first part of the year compared to the previous year, as [ARMED GROUP] used the tactic to extort and intimidate citizens. While the government also claimed that homicides fell during the year, it removed deaths at the hands of the security forces from its reporting.

---

## G Personal Autonomy and Individual Rights

Freedom of travel within [COUNTRY] is complicated by [ARMED GROUP] activity. The main [ARMED GROUP]s control certain neighborhoods of [COUNTRY] cities, making it dangerous for residents to travel, work, and attend school. The [NGO] has estimated that hundreds of thousands of people have been displaced by violence in recent years.
In a major ruling, the supreme court ruled that the government had failed to protect victims forcibly displaced by violence, and gave the government a deadline to develop policies and legislation to protect and assist victims of displacement. The government did not meet this deadline, and work on this legislation was still underway at the end of the period.
Businesses and private citizens are regularly subject to extortion. This activity affects 90 percent of small businesses, according to a report from the [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP]. According to a report from two [NGO]s, businesses and individuals pay extortion fees worth 1.7 percent of [COUNTRY]'s GDP.
[ETHNIC GROUP] people also face difficulties securing land rights and accessing credit.
Abortion is punishable by imprisonment, including in cases where a woman’s life is at risk due to her pregnancy, and the highest court affirmed that the “rights of the mother cannot be privileged over the fetus.” Some women have been jailed despite credible claims that their pregnancies ended due to miscarriage. A small number of women found their sentences commuted or received acquittals, though this remains rare. In one case, a court commuted the sentences of three women, calling their sentences disproportionate. In another case, a woman previously convicted of murder for her stillbirth and sentenced to 30 years in prison was acquitted. Weeks later, prosecutors announced they would seek a third trial.
Adolescent pregnancy is a serious problem in [COUNTRY]. Adolescent pregnancies account for one out of every three pregnancies, and many are the result of sexual assault.
Domestic and sexual violence remains high. In a report, the [NGO] indicated that sexual violence against women increased by a third. The majority of victims were girls between the ages of 12 and 17. The [NGO] also reported [POPULATION FIGURE] cases of missing women and [POPULATION FIGURE] cases of femicide, down from the previous year.
There were reports of several incidents of femicide-suicide, where women and girls die by suicide as a result of abuse, during the period. [COUNTRY] remains one of the only countries in the world where this is considered a crime, and saw its first conviction.
LGBT+ marriage remains illegal in [COUNTRY], though at least one politician has recently voiced support for the idea. In a major city, a politician openly called on their party to consider its legalization. Transgender rights were slightly expanded when a judge ruled in favor of a transgender woman who sought to update her name and gender on government-issued identification.
[COUNTRY] remains a source, transit, and destination country for the trafficking of women, children, and LGBT+ people. There are instances of forced labor in the construction and informal sectors.
The [NGO] report noted that [COUNTRY] investigated one public official for their involvement in trafficking, and convicted [POPULATION FIGURE] traffickers. The report went on to highlight the country’s services for girls who survived trafficking, but called services for boys, adults, and LGBT+ survivors critically insufficient.
Children are vulnerable to economic exploitation, and child labor is a serious problem. Children perform dangerous jobs in agriculture, and are recruited by [ARMED GROUP]s and other criminal elements to carry out illegal activities. While the government made improvements in collecting and publishing data on this activity, and continued a National Action Plan for the Protection of Children and Adolescents, progress in combating child exploitation was slow.

