*State Department Human Rights Report*

e. Denial of Fair Public Trial

The constitution and law provide for an independent judiciary, but some [NGO]s, international organizations, and legal experts asserted that political pressure and corruption influenced prosecutors and judges. The process of appointing judges and prosecutors remained somewhat politicized, although the law provides for a prosecutorial council to select prosecutors. Inadequate funding and a lack of organization continued to hamper the effectiveness of the courts. The law provides for plea bargaining, which is available for all crimes except war crimes and those related to terrorism.

Trial Procedures

The constitution and law provide for the right to a fair and public trial. By law defendants are presumed innocent. Authorities are required to inform detainees of the grounds for their detention. Defendants have the right to a fair and public trial without undue delay and to be present at their trial. Courts may close certain sessions during the testimony of government-protected or other sensitive witnesses. Authorities also close juvenile trials. Defendants have the right to consult an attorney in a timely manner in pretrial and trial proceedings. The law requires authorities to provide an attorney at public expense when a defendant is a person with disabilities or is already in detention, destitute, facing a charge carrying a possible sentence of more than 10 years, being tried in absentia, or engaged in a plea-bargaining process. Defendants have the right to adequate time and facilities to prepare a defense; to free interpretation from the moment charged through all appeals; and to confront prosecution witnesses, present their own witnesses and evidence, and remain silent. Both the defense and the prosecution have the right of appeal.
While the judiciary endeavored to hold criminal trials publicly, it often did not do so due to a shortage of proper facilities. The shortage also affected the timeliness of trials. Systemic weaknesses, such as political influence and prolonged procedures, diminished public confidence in the efficiency and impartiality of the judiciary.
Courts may try defendants in absentia, but by law must repeat the trial if the convicted individuals are later apprehended.

Political Prisoners and Detainees

There were no reports of political prisoners or detainees.

Civil Judicial Procedures and Remedies

The constitution and law provide for an independent judiciary in civil matters, and citizens had access to courts to bring lawsuits seeking damages for violations of constitutionally recognized human rights. Although parties brought suits alleging human rights violations and at times prevailed, perceptions that the system was subject to nepotism, corruption, and political influence led to widespread public distrust. According to [NGO]s, courts in most cases either rejected civil cases involving claims of human rights violations or proceeded on them slowly. When domestic courts issued decisions pertaining to human rights, the government generally complied with them.
Upon exhausting all other available effective legal remedies, citizens may appeal alleged violations of human rights to the supreme court. A large number of cases filed with the court involved such complaints. The supreme court has the authority to review all alleged constitutional and human rights violations. If it finds a violation, it vacates the lower court’s decision and refers the case to an appropriate court or other authority to rectify the deficiency.
There were also administrative remedies for violations of constitutionally protected human rights. In cases of police abuse, citizens can address complaints to the [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] for civilian control of police operations, which may then make recommendations for action to the chief of police or the interior minister. The ombudsman’s office noted the long duration of trials, especially those that were deemed a high priority, eroded citizens’ trust in the court system. This was particularly pronounced in disputes dealing with the establishment or termination of employment or the right to earnings and other wages. The office was also empowered to act in certain individual cases.
Once national remedies are exhausted, individuals, regardless of citizenship, may appeal cases alleging government violations of human rights to an international human rights court.

Property Restitution

While the government is responsible for [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] restitution issues and has laws and mechanisms in place, the pre-war [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] population was estimated to have been only about [POPULATION FIGURE] individuals with no identified place of worship or communal property. There is one possible case of a claim for restitution regarding war-era properties. A family that has the longest-running property restitution case in [COUNTRY] recently reported its [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] heritage, thus potentially bringing this case under the purview of a relevant declaration. Neither the local [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] community nor the government has thus far confirmed the information.
[COUNTRY]’s restitution law was most recently amended several years ago, and [COUNTRY] has not passed any laws dealing with restitution following the endorsement of a relevant declaration in recent years, nor did it make any special provisions for heirless property from the war era. The passage of a law on the restitution of religious or communal properties would have minimal impact on the [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] community, given its small size and the absence of identified pre-war [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] communal property. Any such legislation would mainly apply to properties confiscated from certain [ETHNIC GROUP]s and [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] groups during a period of authoritarian rule.
A large number of restitution claims for private and religious properties confiscated during a period of authoritarian rule remained unresolved. Both private individuals and [NGO]s criticized the government for delays in addressing this problem.

---

*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]


## Overview

While numerous political parties compete for power in [COUNTRY], the opposition is fragmented and its leaders are frequently harassed, and the governing [RULING PARTY] has been in power for many years. Corruption is a serious issue. Investigative journalists and journalists critical of the government face pressure, as do many [NGO]s.

## Key Developments

In recent years, a businessman and regime insider released secret video-recordings—and later, secret audio-recordings and documents—implicating numerous public figures from the state’s key institutions in bribery scandals. Those implicated include a former mayor of the capital and current advisor to the president, as well as the supreme state prosecutor and former high officials of the central bank.
Beginning that year, the revelations inspired a citizen-led, grassroots wave of antigovernment protests for democratization of the country that was eventually supported by all [OPPOSITION PARTY]s, prominent [NGO]s, and many media outlets. By mid-year, however, the momentum was lost and protests gradually dissipated.
Two leaders of the [OPPOSITION PARTY] charged with plotting an attempted coup were found guilty and given sentences of five years in prison. The legal proceedings throughout the case were marked by confusion and opacity.
Later that year, after more than 100 opposition amendments were rejected, a controversial bill regulating religious property was adopted. Its measures targeting exclusively a [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] group, the main denomination in the country, and a wave of road blockades and protest marches swept the country upon its passage.

---

## F Rule of Law

Efforts to bolster judicial independence continue, though the judiciary remains susceptible to pressure from the government, and judicial corruption remains a problem.
Secret audio recordings and official documents that were leaked that year implicated the chief prosecutor and the president of the supreme court in bribery and corruption affairs. Coupled with the sentencing of the [OPPOSITION PARTY] leaders following legal proceedings characterized by confusion and opacity, the events of that year reflected serious deficiencies in transparency, openness, and accountability in the judicial system.
Constitutional guarantees of due process are inconsistently upheld. Legal proceedings are lengthy and often highly bureaucratic, particularly when involving business dealings. Police frequently hold suspects in extended pretrial detention while completing investigations. Courts are poorly funded and often overburdened.
Two [OPPOSITION PARTY] leaders, who were charged with plotting an attempted coup several years ago were found guilty and were each given sentences of up to five years in prison. Legal procedures surrounding the trial were chaotic and opaque, several witnesses recanted testimony, and many details of the alleged plot remained murky after the trial closed with numerous convictions. Aspects of the long-running case were denounced by the [OPPOSITION PARTY] as attempts by the [RULING PARTY] to bolster its dominant political position, and overall the affair reflected a lack of due process and adherence to proper procedures in criminal matters.

---

## C Functioning of Government

The president has wielded vast personalized power for decades through his tenure as both prime minister and president, as well as during his time outside of government as chair of the [RULING PARTY]. He maintains extensive control over most public institutions. Although the constitution provides for a parliamentary system of government, the parliament passed a new law after the president's election that year that greatly expanded presidential powers, including by allowing the president to form councils, committees, and working groups. Critics claim that the changes could amount to a de facto move toward a semipresidential system of government.
The parliament remains weak and has limited capacity to exercise its oversight functions. Opposition boycotts of the parliament have further diminished the power of the legislative branch to act as a check.
Corruption and cronyism remain widespread, and modest efforts by authorities to address the problem, prompted in part by [NGO] accession requirements, have not produced significant results. A new anticorruption agency began its work several years ago, but a report published by [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] questioned the credibility and independence of the agency, in addition to its priority-setting, and noted the continued prevalence of high-level corruption and an overall lack of independence of various public institutions. Senior officials implicated in corruption schemes rarely face prosecution. [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] and independent media provide some accountability by reporting on official corruption and its effects.
In recent years, revelations of corrupt activities of public figures continued to reflect deep systemic problems. In [CITY], a businessman and regime insider released secret video recordings—and later, secret audio recordings and documents—implicating numerous public figures from the state’s key institutions in bribery scandals. Those implicated in wrongdoing include a former mayor of the capital and current advisor to the president; as well as the supreme state prosecutor; and former high officials of the central bank.

[Reidentification] Actual: Montenegro (MNE) → WRONG
1. Bulgaria
2. Serbia
3. Romania
Reason: The combination of a dominant ruling party, a weak and fragmented opposition, widespread corruption, and a judiciary susceptible to government pressure, along with the specific details about property restitution and religious minority issues, suggests a country in Eastern Europe with a complex post-communist transition, which is most closely matched by Bulgaria.

