*State Department Human Rights Report*

a. Freedom of Expression, Including for the Press

Although the constitution and law provide for freedom of speech and press, the government has extensive legal powers to restrict media activities. The government restricted journalistic activity through prepublication censorship. Media remained weak and under government influence or control. Persons close to the president, including his relatives, owned the few private media outlets that existed. Most journalists practiced self-censorship. Those who did not were subject to government surveillance, arrests, and threats.
Freedom of Expression : Individuals generally chose not to criticize the president, his family, other high-ranking officials, and the security forces due to fear of reprisal. The government attempted to impede criticism by continuing to monitor the activities of [OPPOSITION PARTY] members, journalists, and others.
The government owned the only national radio and television broadcast system. The vice president owned the only private broadcast media. Journalists who worked for these entities could not report freely. During recent elections, the government censored all international channels.
The government denied or left pending requests by [POLITICAL PARTIES] to establish private radio stations. Satellite broadcasts were widely available, including international television channels, which the government partially owned.
International news agencies did not have correspondents or regular stringers in the country. As most foreigners need visas to visit the country, the time-consuming nature of the process effectively dissuaded some journalists from travelling, although international media covered major events. In other cases, the government may have prevented reporters from obtaining visas.
Violence and Harassment : The security forces detained, intimidated, and harassed journalists. The government took no steps to preserve the safety and independence of media or to prosecute individuals who harassed journalists.
In a major city, the police arrested two journalists, employees of a private TV station, and held them without charge for several weeks, before releasing them from jail and firing them from their jobs. The arrests were retaliation for an interview with a judge suspended by the president of the supreme court.
Libel/Slander Laws : The government used laws against libel and slander, both of which are criminalized, to restrict public discussion.

Internet Freedom

The government restricted and disrupted access to the internet and censored online content. During recent elections, the government blocked all access to the internet for approximately 10 days.
Access to social media platforms and [OPPOSITION PARTY] blogs continued to be generally restricted.
Users attempting to access [OPPOSITION PARTY] sites were redirected to the government’s official press website or received a message that the websites did not exist. Messaging apps and the internet were the primary ways that the [OPPOSITION PARTY] expressed and disseminated their views.

Academic Freedom and Cultural Events

The government restricted academic freedom and cultural events. Faculty, students, and members of [OPPOSITION PARTY] complained of government interference in the hiring of teachers, the employment of unqualified teachers, and official pressure on teachers to give passing grades to failing students with political connections. Teachers with political connections but no experience or accreditation were employed and reportedly seldom appeared at the classes they were assigned to teach. Most professors practiced self-censorship. Recently, the minister of education fired a teacher from [OPPOSITION PARTY], allegedly because he was promoting his political ideology in his classes. [OPPOSITION PARTY] blogs alleged the teacher was fired because he criticized a rule requiring female students to cut their hair to a certain length.
Some cultural events required coordination with the Ministry of Information or the Department of Culture, which was more common outside of the capital. The resulting bureaucratic delay was a disincentive for prospective organizers, who often did not know the criteria used for judging proposals or their chances for approval.

---

