*State Department Human Rights Report*

e. Denial of Fair Public Trial

Although the constitution and law provide for an independent judiciary, court decisions were susceptible to improper influence by both private and public entities, particularly at the local level, as well as by [ARMED GROUP]s. Authorities sometimes failed to respect court orders, and arrest warrants were sometimes ignored. Across the criminal justice system, many actors lacked the necessary training and resources to carry out their duties fairly and consistently in line with the principle of equal justice.

Trial Procedures

In recent years, all civilian and military courts officially transitioned from an inquisitorial legal system based primarily upon judicial review of written documents to an accusatorial trial system reliant upon oral testimony presented in open court. In most areas, alternative justice centers employed mechanisms such as mediation, negotiation, and restorative justice to resolve minor offenses outside the court system.
Under the accusatorial system, judges conduct all hearings and trials and follow the principles of public access and cross-examination. Defendants have the right to a presumption of innocence and to a fair and public trial without undue delay. Defendants have the right to attend the hearings and to challenge the evidence or testimony presented. Defendants may not be compelled to testify or confess guilt. The law also provides for the rights of appeal and of bail in most categories of crimes. Defendants have the right to an attorney of their choice at all stages of criminal proceedings. By law attorneys are required to meet professional qualifications to represent a defendant. Not all public defenders were qualified, however, and often the public defender system was understaffed. Administration of public defender services was the responsibility of either the judicial or the executive branch, depending on the jurisdiction. According to [NGO], most criminal suspects did not receive representation until after their first custody hearing, thus making individuals vulnerable to coercion to sign false statements prior to appearing before a judge.
Defendants have the right to free assistance of an interpreter, if needed, although interpretation and translation services into [ETHNIC GROUP] languages were not always available. [ETHNIC GROUP] defendants who did not speak the dominant language sometimes were unaware of the status of their cases and were convicted without fully understanding the documents they were instructed to sign.
The lack of national rules of evidence caused confusion and led to disparate judicial rulings.

Political Prisoners and Detainees

There were no reports of political prisoners or detainees.

Civil Judicial Procedures and Remedies

Citizens have access to an independent judiciary in civil matters to seek civil remedies for human rights violations. For a plaintiff to secure damages against a defendant, authorities first must find the defendant guilty in a criminal case, a significant barrier due to the relatively low number of criminal convictions.

---

*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]


## Overview

[COUNTRY] has been an electoral democracy for many years, and alternation in power between parties is routine at both the federal and state levels. However, the country suffers from severe rule of law deficits that limit full citizen enjoyment of political rights and civil liberties. Violence perpetrated by organized criminals, corruption among government officials, human rights abuses by both state and nonstate actors, and rampant impunity are among the most visible of [COUNTRY]’s many governance challenges.

## Key Developments

The president, who took office a few years ago, maintained high approval ratings through much of the year, and the [RULING PARTY] consolidated its grasp on power in recent elections. The president’s polling position began to wane late in the year, as [COUNTRY]’s dire security situation affected voters’ views on performance.
In recent months, the government created a new gendarmerie, the [security force], which officially began operating after drawing from the military and police forces. Rights advocates criticized the agency, warning that its creation deepened the militarization of public security.
The number of deaths attributed to organized crime remained at historic highs, though the rate of acceleration slowed. Massacres of police officers, alleged criminals, and civilians were well-publicized as the year progressed. The challenge to state authority was illustrated dramatically when soldiers and police were forced to release an accused crime leader in a major city after a violent running battle with [ARMED GROUP] members.
[COUNTRY] bowed to external pressure and agreed to detain, deter, and deport asylum seekers and migrants traveling through [COUNTRY] to its border with a neighboring country, after the leader of that country threatened to impose punitive tariffs. The government ordered nearly half the new [security force] to enforce this policy, partially diverting it from its crime-fighting mandate.

---

## F Rule of Law

[COUNTRY]'s justice system is plagued by delays, unpredictability, and corruption, leading to impunity. A constitutional reform replaced the civil-inquisitorial trial system with an oral-adversarial one. Although implementation has slowly proceeded and some elements of due process have improved, human rights groups have raised concerns about ongoing deficiencies, including questions about judicial impartiality and training. Implementation of the new system was technically completed, but deficient training and a lack of commitment to the initiative by authorities have produced poor prosecutorial results. The supreme court is generally regarded as independent, but several appointments of justices viewed as close to the government raised concerns about diminished autonomy. One appointment was to replace a justice who resigned while under investigation for corruption.

Lower courts—and law enforcement in general—are undermined by widespread bribery and suffer from limited capacity. According to a government survey, the vast majority of crimes committed went unreported, in large part because the underpaid police were viewed as either inept or in league with criminals. Even when investigations are conducted, only a handful of crimes end in convictions.

[COUNTRY]'s population is subject to the threat of violence at the hands of several actors, including individual criminals, [ARMED GROUP]s that operate with impunity, and police officers who are often susceptible to bribery. The population is particularly vulnerable to enforced disappearances, which remains a tremendous challenge for the government despite efforts to address the missing-persons backlog in recent years. In addition, individuals in police custody are at risk of torture by the authorities, and must also navigate a prison system where due process and physical safety are in short supply.

Abuses during criminal investigations are rife; a UN special rapporteur released a report characterizing torture as “generalized” within [COUNTRY]'s police forces. Legislation passed established a prohibition on the use of torture and disqualified evidence obtained through its use. Rights advocates suggest it has contributed to mild progress in excluding torture-based confessions from prosecutions, but remain concerned about gaps in implementation.

Human rights advocates have consistently expressed concern about a lack of accountability for rights abuses committed by members of the military, including torture, forced disappearances, and extrajudicial executions. Only a handful of soldiers have been convicted in civilian courts for abuses against civilians. In recent years, a [NGO] has gained the government some credibility on this subject, in part by issuing reports implicating state security forces in grave human rights abuses. The appointment of a close ally of the president as the [NGO]'s new leader generated fears for the agency’s independence. The Office of the UN High Commissioner for Human Rights also voiced concerns for human rights in [COUNTRY], criticizing the government for its inability to adopt a national program on torture and criticizing the use of soldiers in public security operations.

Forced disappearances and killings remain a pervasive problem in [COUNTRY], despite recent efforts to combat the problem. Legislation passed removed the statute of limitations on missing-persons crimes and established a national mechanism for investigating such cases, which numbered over [POPULATION FIGURE] as recently as a few months prior. A [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] announced the discovery of over [POPULATION FIGURE] bodies in graves throughout the country when it released its first tally.

The kidnapping and presumed deaths of [POPULATION FIGURE] students in a rural area remains controversial, with the president creating a presidential commission to investigate the case after taking office. More assistance came when the UN High Commissioner for Human Rights agreed to support the presidential commission. Nevertheless, the government was unable to close the matter. Instead, at least [POPULATION FIGURE] of the [POPULATION FIGURE] detained in connection with disappearances were released, often because they were subject to torture while in custody.

As in previous years, the government’s primary response to insecurity was the deployment of militarized forces to hotspots. The president introduced a new security force that would draw from the military and federal police, and would rely on military officers for its top ranks. The new security force was sharply criticized by rights advocates, fearing it would lead to the militarization of public security. [NGO] also criticized its enabling legislation, for allowing the security force to determine whether any encountered protests are of “legitimate purpose” and whether to respond with force.

Nevertheless, the number of deaths attributed to organized crime remained at historic highs, though the rate of acceleration slowed. Violence remained acute in several regions. Several mass killings sparked widespread outcry, including the murder of [POPULATION FIGURE] people attending a family party in a city, the deaths of at least [POPULATION FIGURE] patrons at a bar set ablaze, and the deaths of [POPULATION FIGURE] state police officers.

An effort to capture a [MILITANT GROUP] leader in a state capital caused particular controversy. After a security patrol captured the leader, they were surrounded by heavily armed [MILITANT GROUP] members, who overpowered the patrol and forced them to surrender the leader. At least [POPULATION FIGURE] people were killed and [POPULATION FIGURE] prisoners escaped in the fighting, generating widespread anguish over the government’s failure to keep the city secure.

The killing of [POPULATION FIGURE] members of a [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] family garnered attention. The family has been targeted by local [ARMED GROUP]s for at least a decade. By the end of the year, federal prosecutors detained [POPULATION FIGURE] suspects in the killings, one of them a local police chief allegedly in the pay of a [MILITANT GROUP].

The year closed with another violent running battle, this time in a town. [MILITANT GROUP] members arrived in armored trucks and attacked the town hall, prompting a military counterattack. When the battle ended, at least [POPULATION FIGURE] were dead, including [POPULATION FIGURE] fighters and [POPULATION FIGURE] civilians kidnapped by the [MILITANT GROUP].

[COUNTRY]'s prisons remain unsafe, with inmates commonly engaging in criminal activity while incarcerated. Inmates affiliated with a [MILITANT GROUP] orchestrated a string of violence from their prison; [POPULATION FIGURE] people were subsequently murdered in a two-week span. A prison was closed, but inmates remain at risk elsewhere. In a jail, [POPULATION FIGURE] inmates died when a fire swept through.

Several alternative approaches to the violence epidemic, including amnesty for low-level offenders, an end to the strategy of targeting [MILITANT GROUP] leaders, and drug law reform, remained under discussion.

[COUNTRY]'s law bans discrimination based on ethnic origin, gender, age, religion, and sexual orientation. Nevertheless, the large [ETHNIC GROUP] population has been subject to social and economic discrimination, and at least [POPULATION FIGURE] percent of [ETHNIC GROUP] people live in poverty. Regions with high concentrations of [ETHNIC GROUP] residents suffer from particularly deficient services. [ETHNIC GROUP] communities have been harmed by criminal violence. In recent years, communities have formed self-defense groups, some of which were subsequently legalized.

[COUNTRY]'s law has strong protections for LGBT+ people, but they are not uniformly enforced. Transgender women in particular face discrimination and violence.

Migrants from neighboring countries have long faced persecution and criminal predation. The government initially sought to protect migrants, but reversed course when another country threatened to seal its border with [COUNTRY] and institute punitive tariffs. In response, [COUNTRY] deployed its security forces to arrest the northern flow of migrants, established immigration checkpoints along major roads, and raided migrant shelters in the country.

The government also cooperated with the implementation of a policy that forces asylum seekers to remain in [COUNTRY] until their cases are processed. As a result, as many as [POPULATION FIGURE] have been forced to wait in border cities for their hearings, finding themselves at risk of kidnapping and extortion in the interim.

---

## C Functioning of Government

Organized crime and related violence have limited the effective governing authority of elected officials in some areas of [COUNTRY]. Members of organized crime groups have persisted in their attempts to infiltrate local governments in order to plunder municipal coffers and ensure their own impunity. A notorious and still unsolved mass disappearance of [POPULATION FIGURE] students in [CITY] was linked to a deeply corrupt local government collaborating with a [MILITANT GROUP], as well as a group of corrupt soldiers residing in a nearby barracks. In the most violent regions, the provision of public services has become more difficult, as public-sector employees face extortion and pressure to divert public funds.

Official corruption remains a serious problem. The billions of dollars in illegal drug money that enter [COUNTRY] each year profoundly affect politics, as does rampant public-contract fraud and other forms of siphoning off state funds. Attempts to prosecute officials for alleged involvement in corrupt or criminal activity have often failed due to the weakness of the cases brought by the state.

The administration of the president has pursued corruption more actively than recent administrations, and [NGO] recorded a slight improvement in citizens’ perceptions of corruption in recent years. Notably, the [NGO] also found that 34 percent of study participants reported paying a bribe for public services, while 51 percent reported the same several years prior. Several prominent figures from the previous administration were also indicted or arrested on graft charges. In recent months, an arrest warrant was issued for the former head of the state oil company, along with his relatives. He remained free, but one of his relatives was arrested abroad and agreed to return to [COUNTRY] to mount a legal defense. In addition, a former urban development minister was arrested on corruption charges; the minister was suspected of wrongdoing throughout their tenure in the previous administration.

As the year progressed, the former president himself was accused of accepting bribes, notably in proceedings taking place abroad. A [MILITANT GROUP] leader testified in a foreign court that the former president accepted a bribe from a notorious [MILITANT GROUP] leader, who was then on trial for murder. In recent months, the securities regulator received an allegation that the former president accepted bribes to allow the state oil company to acquire a business at an inflated price.

In recent months, a feared former public security secretary was charged with trafficking cocaine abroad; the secretary was often accused of collaborating with a [MILITANT GROUP] during their tenure. However, critics have charged the government with selective prosecution, pointing to the administration’s absolution of a veteran politician following allegations of questionable asset accumulation.

Despite some limitations, several freedom of information laws passed in recent years have successfully strengthened transparency at the federal level, though enforcement is uneven across states. In recent years, the government has failed to release relevant information on some of [COUNTRY]’s most controversial issues, including abuses by the security forces and the investigation into a major disappearance in [CITY]. Since several years ago, [COUNTRY] authorities have also remained sluggish in publicizing contracts with a foreign firm that sold spyware used to target journalists, lawyers, and activists. In recent months, a privacy watchdog accused the federal attorney general of stonewalling its investigation for over a year.

However, in recent months, transparency advocates succeeded in obtaining a list of beneficiaries of tax amnesties; the status of many beneficiaries and the large amounts forgiven prompted allegations of politicized tax practices by previous administrations.

