*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]


## Overview

While regular multiparty elections have taken place for many years, [COUNTRY]'s politics have been controlled for decades by the president's family, with the current president and their predecessor dominating the political landscape. Advantages including security services dominated by the president's ethnic group and malapportioned election districts have helped the president and their party retain power. Opposition calls for constitutional and electoral reforms have been harshly repressed for several years.

## Key Developments

The new parliament, which was dominated by the [RULING PARTY] due to an [OPPOSITION PARTY] boycott of recent elections, voted overwhelmingly to adopt a constitutional amendment that reinstated the two-term limit for the presidency. The rule did not apply retroactively, however, meaning the president could remain in power for many years to come.
In recent years, local council elections were held for the first time in several decades, with the [RULING PARTY] capturing a majority of the seats.
The authorities continued to restrict protests, and the parliament passed legislation that imposed additional limits on freedom of assembly.

---

## D Freedom of Expression and Belief

Freedom of the press is guaranteed in the constitution but inconsistently upheld in practice. Numerous independent media outlets offer a variety of viewpoints, but a history of impunity for those who commit crimes against journalists, as well as restrictive press laws, encourage self-censorship. There is no mechanism to appeal decisions made by the regulatory body for broadcasting and communication, which can suspend outlets for violations of broadly worded regulations. Recently, the regulatory body revoked the license of a newspaper for “deliberate refusal to respect the fundamental principles of journalism.”
The security forces have engaged in violence and other acts of intimidation to discourage press coverage of opposition protests that began several years ago. Authorities have also temporarily disrupted mobile phone and internet service during protests, hampering efforts to report on them.
Religious freedom is constitutionally protected and generally respected in practice. A major religion, as well as a couple of other prominent faiths, are recognized by the state; other groups must register as religious associations to receive similar benefits. The registration process has been subject to long delays and a large backlog; approximately [POPULATION FIGURE] applications were pending recently.
Academics are generally able to engage in political discussions. However, the security forces have repeatedly used violence and arrests to quell student protests.
Citizens are able to speak openly in private discussion, but they may be arrested on incitement or other charges for speaking critically about the government to journalists or [NGO].
Recently, the parliament adopted legislation that criminalizes publication of false information and breaches of public morality, among other problematic provisions that could affect online freedom of expression. The law also granted the security forces greater authority to conduct electronic surveillance.

