## [COUNTRY]


## Overview

[COUNTRY]'s vibrant and competitive democratic system has allowed several peaceful transfers of power between rival parties for many years, and protections for civil liberties are generally robust. Ongoing concerns include foreign migrant workers’ vulnerability to exploitation and external efforts to influence policymaking, the media, and [COUNTRY]'s democratic infrastructure.

## Key Developments

A law enacted that year allowed same-sex partners to be married, making [COUNTRY] the first in the region to authorize such marriages.
In recent years, ahead of national elections, the legislature passed new legislation that imposes criminal penalties for illegal foreign involvement in lobbying, electoral campaigns, or election-related disinformation in [COUNTRY].

---

## D Freedom of Expression and Belief

The news media reflect a diversity of views and report aggressively on government policies, though many outlets display strong party affiliation in their coverage. The capital continues to exert influence on the media in [COUNTRY]. Key media owners have significant business interests in neighboring countries or rely on advertising by companies from those countries, leaving them vulnerable to pressure and prone to self-censorship on topics considered sensitive by the government. The communications regulatory body fined a number of television news channels that year for airing false reports. Earlier, the regulatory body proposed legislation meant to strengthen safeguards against media monopolies, and the cabinet has proposed draft measures that would make it a criminal offense to create and disseminate “fake news.”
People of all faiths in [COUNTRY] can worship freely. Religious organizations that choose to register with the government receive tax-exempt status.
Educators in [COUNTRY] can generally write and lecture without interference, and past practices—including prosecutions—aimed at restricting academics’ political activism have been rare in recent years.
In a recent month, a political science professor was reportedly summoned for questioning over an online video in which he accused the [RULING PARTY] of pursuing ideologically driven museum policies; the museum under discussion said the claims were false.
Private discussion is open and free, and there were no reports of the government illegally monitoring online communication that year. The government does not restrict internet access.

