*State Department Human Rights Report*

e. Denial of Fair Public Trial

The constitution and law provide for an independent judiciary, but courts remained vulnerable to political pressure, bribery, and intimidation.

Trial Procedures

The law provides for the right to counsel, equal protection under the law, protection from double jeopardy, protection from retroactive application of the law, public trials, and the right to be present at one’s own trial, but these rights were not always upheld. Defendants enjoy the presumption of innocence, except in some cases, such as human trafficking and drug trafficking, where the burden of proof is on the defendant once the charge sheet establishes a prima facie criminal violation. The law provides detainees the right to legal representation and a court-appointed lawyer, a government lawyer, or access to private attorneys. The government provided legal counsel to indigent detainees only upon request. Persons who are unaware of their rights, in particular certain ethnic minority communities and members of some [ETHNIC GROUP], are thus at risk of being deprived of legal representation. Defense lawyers reported having insufficient time to prepare their defense. A supreme court directive ordered that the courts must provide free interpretation services to those who do not speak the dominant language, and interpreters were made available to interpret a variety of languages. Defense lawyers may cross-examine accusers. All lower-court decisions, including acquittals, are subject to appeal. The supreme court is the court of last resort.

Military courts adjudicate cases concerning military personnel under the military code, which provides military personnel the same basic rights as civilians. The military code requires that soldiers accused of rape or homicide be transferred to civilian authorities for prosecution. Under normal circumstances the military prosecutes all other criminal cases raised against soldiers under the military justice system. Nevertheless, the military has told the government it is willing to cooperate with [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] and [NGO]. Military courts cannot try civilians for crimes, even if the crimes involve the military services; civilian courts handle these cases.

Political Prisoners and Detainees

There were no reports of political prisoners or detainees.

Civil Judicial Procedures and Remedies

Individuals or organizations could seek remedies for human rights violations in the national courts.

Property Restitution

[ARMED GROUP] and their affiliate organizations have returned some previously seized property as required by legislation passed that year to end the conflict, but they kept other illegally seized lands and properties. According to [NGO], a significant number of conflict-era land and other property disputes remained outstanding, particularly in rural areas.

---

Section 4. Corruption and Lack of Transparency in Government

The law provides criminal penalties for corruption by officials, but the government did not implement the law effectively. Officials sometimes engaged in corrupt practices with impunity, and there were reports of government corruption during the year.
Corruption : Early in the year a commissioner at the main anticorruption agency, the country’s chief anticorruption body, resigned after a video leaked of his accepting a bribe to resolve an ongoing case concerning privatization of a public educational institution. After the resignation the main prosecution office advised the anticorruption agency to investigate its former commissioner. Shortly thereafter the anticorruption agency lodged a corruption case against the former commissioner. The agency sought to fine and punish the former commissioner under the main anticorruption law. The case is pending before a special court.
The anticorruption agency expanded its investigative scope to include a civil engineering lab to determine the quality of materials used in public infrastructure, a common target for systemic corrupt cost cutting. During the year the anticorruption agency conducted 97 sting operations which facilitated the arrest of 154 civil servants, including a senior civil servant.
As in previous years, student and labor groups associated with [RULING PARTY] and [OPPOSITION PARTY] demanded contributions from schools and businesses. Corruption remained problematic within the security forces.
Financial Disclosure : Public officials are subject to financial disclosure laws and the vast majority of civil servants complied with the requirement. Despite the required financial disclosures, the main oversight body, the entity mandated to monitor financial disclosures and make them publicly available, generally did not systematically review its findings or publish these details.

---

*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]


## Overview

Since the end of a decade-long civil war, [COUNTRY] has held a series of competitive elections and adopted a permanent constitution. As politics have stabilized, pressure on journalists has decreased, and authorities have been more tolerant of peaceful assembly. However, political protests are still occasionally marred by violence, and corruption remains endemic in politics, government, and the judicial system. Other problems include gender-based violence, underage marriage, and bonded labor. Transitional justice bodies have struggled to fulfill their mandates.

## Key Developments

The leader of [OPPOSITION PARTY] agreed to refrain from supporting an independent state in a region after he was released from detention. [OPPOSITION PARTY], renamed, endorsed his agreement. 
In recent years, an anticorruption agency commissioner resigned after a video of him accepting a bribe was publicized; he then became the subject of a corruption case and a money laundering investigation. Both matters were ongoing.
The speaker of the parliament resigned from his post after a parliamentary employee accused him of rape. Authorities arrested him, and his case remained pending.
The parliament considered draft legislation that would create new media regulators and replace an existing cybercrime law, despite concerns from [NGO] that they would restrict speech. The bills remained under consideration.

---

## F Rule of Law

The constitution provides for an independent judiciary. However, judicial independence is compromised by endemic corruption in many courts.
The state has generally ignored local court verdicts, supreme court decisions, and [NGO] recommendations addressing crimes committed during a prolonged civil conflict. The government also sought to weaken the [NGO] with a proposed amendment to human rights law; the amendment would give the attorney general the power to bring human rights cases and would prohibit the [NGO] from opening regional and local offices. The amendment remained under consideration.
Constitutional due process guarantees are poorly upheld in practice. Arbitrary arrests do occur. Heavy case backlogs and a slow appeals process result in long pretrial detentions. The government provides legal counsel to those who cannot afford their own, but only at a defendant’s request. Those unaware of this right often end up representing themselves.
Rights advocates continue to criticize the government for failing to punish abuses and war crimes committed during the conflict.
Due to a lack of will on the part of the security forces and political parties, neither the main transitional justice body nor the other key body have implemented reforms demanded by the international community or the supreme court. The mandates of both transitional bodies were extended, though the terms of its members expired. Although these bodies have received thousands of reports of human rights violations and enforced disappearances, no alleged perpetrators have been prosecuted.
The constitution includes rights for sexual minorities. The first passport on which the holder was permitted to select a third gender was issued. However, LGBT+ people reportedly face continued harassment by the authorities and other citizens, particularly in rural areas.
The constitution frames the protection of fundamental human rights for citizens only. This potentially leaves equal rights of noncitizens, including migrants and people who cannot prove citizenship, unprotected.
A certain ethnic group in [COUNTRY] face difficulty achieving formal refugee status due to pressure from [COUNTRY]'s government. Women often do not receive the same educational and employment opportunities as men.
Children living with disabilities are sometimes excluded from the education system, or face segregation in the classroom.

---

## C Functioning of Government

[COUNTRY] ratified a new constitution, an important step in its democratic transition. Successful legislative elections were held, with new lawmakers seated. However, despite democratic improvements and political stabilization, representative rule is not fully consolidated.
Corruption is endemic in [COUNTRY] politics and government and often goes unpunished. Corruption by officials obstructed the delivery of foreign aid provided to [COUNTRY] after a major earthquake, though aid is slowly being distributed to survivors.
[COUNTRY]'s top anticorruption agency has been more active in recent years. It accused a former director of a tax department of embezzlement and granting improper tax exemptions; the case was still ongoing. 
The agency has been affected by corruption within its ranks, however. The security chief of the agency resigned after a film of him accepting a bribe was made public. The agency filed a corruption case against him, while a money laundering investigation was launched; those cases were pending. The agency accused one of its inspectors of corruption, after he was discovered accepting a bribe to settle a complaint.
The government generally operates with opacity. The election commission, the truth and reconciliation commission, and the commission of investigation on enforced disappeared persons, among others, have been criticized for lack of transparency. Mechanisms for utilizing the right to information law are poorly defined, and the law is inconsistently enforced.

