*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]


## Overview

Political rights and civil liberties are generally respected in [COUNTRY]. There are weaknesses in the rule of law, and corruption remains a serious concern, but recent governments have taken steps to address it. Violence against women is also a significant problem.

## Key Developments

The [RULING PARTY] and another major party were the parliament’s two largest immediately after a major election, winning 8 seats each, while independents won 21. Days after the election, a new governing coalition coalesced around a relaunched party of the former prime minister. 
The prime minister won a fourth nonconsecutive term as premier after being selected by the parliament. A leader of a major party attempted to halt the selection in the courts, but the legal efforts were rejected. 
In recent years, [COUNTRY] ended its diplomatic recognition of a certain country, shifting it to another country. A group of MPs, including a former prime minister, were dismissed from the cabinet after abstaining on the government’s decision.

---

## G Personal Autonomy and Individual Rights

Residents generally enjoy freedom of movement, but some impediments exist, particularly in parts of rural areas where people from a certain ethnic group were expelled during a period of unrest. Hostility to the settlement of this group also persists in parts of a major province.
The legal and regulatory framework largely supports property ownership and private business activity. However, property rights are frequently contested. A major political organization has had its administrative buildings attacked on several occasions. Logging concessions have been disputed by local groups, as have tourism operations.
Individual freedoms on personal status issues such as marriage and divorce are generally protected. However, the legal age of marriage is 15, and about a fifth of women are married by age 18. Legislation passed that year, which formally criminalized domestic violence and enabled victims to apply for protection orders, has been implemented, and the police have received training on how to interact with victims and handle cases. Nevertheless, domestic violence and rape are serious and underreported problems, and there is a reluctance among many victims who do report offenses to take their cases to court.
Legal protections against exploitative working conditions are not consistently enforced, though the authorities have made efforts to update and implement laws against human trafficking in recent years. Local and foreign women and children are vulnerable to sex trafficking and domestic servitude, including through forced marriages or “adoptions” to pay off debts. Migrant workers sometimes face forced labor in the mining, logging, and fishing industries.

## On [COUNTRY]

