*State Department Human Rights Report*

Executive Summary

[COUNTRY] is a multiparty parliamentary democracy. Although it has no constitution, the parliament, a unicameral [POPULATION FIGURE]-member legislative body, has enacted a series of “Basic Laws” that enumerate fundamental rights. Certain fundamental laws, orders, and regulations legally depend on the existence of a “state of emergency,” which has been in effect for decades. Under the Basic Laws, the parliament has the power to dissolve the government and mandate elections. Following nationwide elections, which were generally considered free and fair, [RULING PARTY] and [OPPOSITION PARTY] failed to form a coalition government. Therefore, the parliament voted to dissolve itself and set a date for a third general election within a year.
Under the authority of the prime minister, the security agency combats terrorism and espionage in [COUNTRY], the surrounding territories, and a major city. The national police, including the border police and the immigration police, are under the authority of the ministry of public security. The defense forces are responsible for external security but also have some domestic security responsibilities and report to the ministry of defense. Security forces operating in the surrounding territories fall under the defense forces for operations and operational debriefing. Civilian authorities maintained effective control over the security services.
Significant human rights issues included: reports of unlawful or arbitrary killings, including targeted killings of civilians and soldiers; arbitrary detention; restrictions on certain ethnic minority communities residing in the capital, including arbitrary or unlawful interference with privacy, family, and home; and significant restrictions on freedom of movement.
The government took steps to prosecute and punish officials who committed abuses within [COUNTRY] regardless of rank or seniority.
This section includes [COUNTRY], including the capital. A foreign government recognized the capital of [COUNTRY] in recent years and recognized [COUNTRY]'s sovereignty over a disputed territory. It is the position of that foreign government that the specific boundaries of [COUNTRY]'s sovereignty in the capital are subject to final status negotiations between the parties. A [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] exercises no authority over the capital.
As stated in an appendix, this report contains data drawn from foreign government officials; victims of alleged human rights violations and abuses; academic and congressional studies; and reports from the press, international organizations, and [NGO] concerned with human rights. In the context of the conflict, some of those sources have been accused of harboring political motivations. The [COUNTRY]'s state department assesses external reporting carefully but does not conduct independent investigations in all cases. We have sought and received input from the government of [COUNTRY] and we have noted responses where applicable.

---

f. Arbitrary or Unlawful Interference with Privacy, Family, Home, or Correspondence

The law prohibits such actions, and the government generally respected those prohibitions.
Legislation passed that year, which is renewed annually, prohibits non-[ETHNIC GROUP] individuals from certain countries, including those who are spouses of [COUNTRY] residents or citizens, from obtaining resident status unless the interior minister makes a special determination, usually on humanitarian grounds. The government has extended the law annually due to government reports that family reunification allows entry to a disproportionate number of persons who are later involved in acts of terrorism. [NGO] asserted that statistics from government documents obtained through freedom of information requests contradicted these terrorism allegations, and the denial of residency to certain individuals for the purposes of family reunification led to cases of family separation.
According to [NGO] reports from recent years, there were approximately [POPULATION FIGURE] individuals from certain areas living in [COUNTRY], including the capital, on temporary stay permits because of the law, with no legal guarantee that they would be able to continue living with their families. There were also cases of spouses living in a major city without legal status. Authorities did not permit individuals who were abroad during a major conflict or whose residency permits the government subsequently withdrew to reside permanently in the capital. [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] and other human rights organizations called on the government to repeal this law and resume processing family unification applications. The law allows the entry of spouses of [COUNTRY] citizens on a “staying permit” if the male spouse is age 35 or older and the female spouse is age 25 or older, but they may not receive residency based on their marriage and have no path to citizenship.

---

a. Freedom of Expression, Including for the Press

The law generally provides for freedom of expression, including for the press, and the government generally respected this right. An independent press, an effective judiciary, and a functioning democratic political system combined to promote freedom of expression, including for the press.
The law imposes tort liability on any person who knowingly issues a public call for an economic, cultural, or academic boycott of [COUNTRY] or of institutions or entities in areas under its control. Plaintiffs must prove direct economic harm to claim damages under the “anti-boycott” legislation. The law also permits the finance minister to impose administrative sanctions on those calling for such a boycott, including restrictions on participating in tenders for contracts with the government and denial of government benefits.
In recent years, the parliament passed an amendment barring entry to the country of visitors who called for boycotts, and the Ministry of Strategic Affairs published a list of [POPULATION FIGURE] organizations whose members would be refused entry to [COUNTRY]. The government also used this law to deport the director of [NGO] and [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP], and held them in administrative detention for several years.
Freedom of Expression : The law prohibits hate speech and content liable to incite to violence or discrimination on grounds of race, origin, religion, nationality, and gender.
The maximum penalty for desecrating the national flag is [POPULATION FIGURE] years in prison and a fine of [POPULATION FIGURE].
In cases of speech that are defined as incitement to violence or hate speech, the law empowers police to limit freedom of expression.
A recent law “prohibit[s] individuals or organizations that are not part of the education system from engaging in activities within an educational institution when the nature of the activity undermines the goals of state education.” Both supporters and opponents of the bill said it targeted [NGO], which described its activities as collecting and publishing “the testimonies of [ETHNIC GROUP] who served in the occupied territories in order to generate public discourse on the reality of the occupation, with the aim of bringing it to an end.” [NGO] criticized the law as a violation of freedom of political expression. As of the end of the period, the Ministry of Education had not issued regulations necessary to implement the law.
Security officials prohibited groups affiliated with [ARMED GROUP] or [REBEL GROUP] from meeting in the capital based on a law banning [REBEL GROUP] from engaging in political, diplomatic, security, or security-related activities in [COUNTRY], including the capital.
Press and Media , Including Online Media : Independent media were active and expressed a wide variety of views without restriction, with a few exceptions.
In recent years, police issued a new regulation regarding the work of journalists in areas experiencing clashes, which authorities claimed balanced freedom of the press and security requirements. According to [NGO], the regulation grants police broad authorities to prevent journalists’ access to public incidents involving violence if there exists a concern that the entry of journalists would lead to “special circumstances,” such as injury or the loss of life, further violence, disrupting investigative procedures, serious violation of privacy, or violation of a closure order. According to the regulation, however, police must also consider alternatives to minimize the violation of press freedom, for instance by escorting journalists in and out of dangerous situations.
Violence and Harassment : Journalists from [ETHNIC GROUP] who were able to obtain entry permits, as well as [CITY]-based [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] journalists, reported incidents of harassment, racism, and occasional violence when they sought to cover news in the capital, especially in the Old City and its vicinity. According to a statement by [NGO], “[RELIGIOUS MINORITY] journalists [are] needlessly hassled by [COUNTRY] security in what we believe is clear ethnic profiling.” This included reports of alleged harassment by [COUNTRY] soldiers and acts of violence against [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] and [ETHNIC GROUP] journalists that prevented them from covering news stories. According to [NGO], [POPULATION FIGURE] [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] journalists were detained in [COUNTRY] prisons as of the end of the period. In recent years, [NGO] issued a statement criticizing the government for holding [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] journalists in its jails, noting “[COUNTRY]’s use of administrative detention to hold journalists without charge runs completely contrary to its professed values of democracy and rule of law.”
The Ministry of Interior sought to deport stateless photojournalist [NAME] from [CITY] to [COUNTRY], after he was unable to obtain residency status in [CITY], and held him in administrative detention for several months. In recent years, [NGO] called on authorities to either clarify the reasons for [NAME]’s detention and deportation order or release him immediately. After [COUNTRY] refused to accept [NAME], a court reviewing border-control decisions released him, due to the Ministry of Interior’s inability to deport him. The court ordered [NAME] to regularize his status by a certain date. While the government classified the reasons for the denial of [NAME]’s status for security reasons, in an appeal of his deportation a Supreme Court justice stated [NAME] “crossed the line between his journalistic work and assisting [ARMED GROUP]” but also mentioned “reports that are not sympathetic to [COUNTRY],” according to [MEDIA OUTLET].
The prime minister and his supporters criticized journalists, media channels, and media owners for reporting on investigations into a series of allegations involving the prime minister, for which the attorney general decided to indict him. In recent years, the [RULING PARTY] published billboards with photographs of four journalists saying, “they will not decide,” according to media reports. Following attacks in media and social media by the prime minister and his son against a journalist who covered the prime minister’s investigations, the journalist received a series of threats on WhatsApp and social media, which led the channel to provide him with a private security guard. On another occasion, the prime minister criticized the heads of a media channel for their coverage of his office, called for a boycott of the channel, and said they were carrying out a “terror attack against democracy,” while treating rival political parties more gently than [RULING PARTY]. The prime minister argued that he was working to increase competition in the domestic television market.
On another occasion, a group of ultra-Orthodox men physically attacked a journalist near a major city. The attackers severely assaulted the journalist, breaking his nose and resulting in a concussion. The attackers called him a “traitor” and a “leftist” after confirming he was a journalist. Police arrested a suspect in the attack, and the investigation of the case was pending as of the end of the period.
On another occasion, the state attorney issued a directive instructing prosecutors to consider requesting increased sentences of [POPULATION FIGURE] years’ imprisonment for violent offenses committed against journalists.
Censorship or Content Restrictions : All media organizations must submit to military censors any material relating to specific military issues or strategic infrastructure problems. Organizations may appeal the censor’s decisions to the Supreme Court, and the censor may not appeal a court judgment.
News printed or broadcast abroad is subject to security censorship. The government regularly enacted restrictive orders on sensitive security information and continuing investigations and required foreign correspondents, as well as local media, to abide by these orders. According to data provided by the armed forces, in recent years the censor intervened in [POPULATION FIGURE] articles and banned [POPULATION FIGURE] articles.
While the government retained the authority to censor the printing of publications for security concerns, anecdotal evidence suggested authorities did not actively review [CITY]-based [LANGUAGE] publications. Those publications, however, reported they engaged in self-censorship.
National Security : The law criminalizes as “terrorist acts” speech supporting terrorism, including public praise of a terrorist organization, display of symbols, expression of slogans, and “incitement.” In recent years, the parliament amended the law to authorize restrictions on the release of bodies of terrorists and their funerals to prevent “incitement to terror or identification with a terrorist organization or an act of terror.” The government issued [POPULATION FIGURE] indictments and courts convicted [POPULATION FIGURE] persons under the law during the period. On another occasion, a court partially accepted the appeal of a [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] individual, who was convicted due to poems, pictures, and other media content she posted online. The court reversed lower court verdicts on charges of “incitement to violence” and “support of a terrorist organization” related to her poetry, but it upheld convictions related to her other publications. The ruling stated that when examining freedom of expression, the fact that the individual’s words were part of an artistic piece had to be taken into consideration.

Internet Freedom

The government monitored electronic communications for security purposes. The law authorizes district court judges to restrict access to internet sites to prevent the commission of crimes. The end-of-period report stated that requests to digital services companies to remove content based on its assessment that the content is illegal under the law led to the removal of [POPULATION FIGURE] online postings. According to the report, [POPULATION FIGURE] percent of the requests were due to offenses related to a terror organization, and [POPULATION FIGURE] percent were due to incitement offenses. On another occasion, [NGO] and [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] petitioned the Supreme Court to stop the Ministry of Justice’s Cyber Unit process of requesting that digital services companies voluntarily remove contents prior to a formal legal review, arguing these requests infringe on the rights of freedom of expression and due process. The state prosecution argued the Cyber Unit approached companies only after investigative authorities identified the contents as violating the law. On another occasion, a district court judge rejected a request by [NGO] to review [POPULATION FIGURE] websites for transparency purposes, arguing that a publication of domains would contradict the reason for their removal.

Academic Freedom and Cultural Events

A law prohibits institutions that receive government funding from engaging in commemoration of the [EVENT], or “catastrophe,” the term used by [ETHNIC GROUP] to refer to the displacement of [ETHNIC GROUP] during [COUNTRY]’s War of Independence. Activities forbidden by the law include rejection of the existence of [COUNTRY] as a “Jewish and democratic state” or commemorating “Independence Day or the day on which the State was established as a day of mourning.”
On another occasion, the Supreme Court issued an injunction demanding that the minister of Science and Technology explain why he blocked the appointment of a professor to an official [COUNTRY]-[COUNTRY] scientific committee. In recent years, the minister had claimed he blocked the appointment because years earlier the professor had signed a petition by faculty members in universities, expressing “support and appreciation to the students and lecturers who refuse to serve as soldiers in the occupied territories.”
On another occasion, following a demand from the minister of Culture and Sports, the mayor of a major city decided not to show a documentary film about an attorney’s defense of [ETHNIC GROUP] prisoners, in the city’s cultural center as a part of a film festival. Following a letter from [NGO] arguing the decision was illegal, the deputy attorney general informed the mayor he had no authority to cancel the showing of a film. In protest [NGO] presented the film in a nearby location and outside the city’s cultural center. In recent years, after the film was selected as the winning film in the festival, [ORGANIZATION] announced it would stop funding the award for the winning film in the festival but later retracted its announcement.
The government maintained prohibitions on some prominent [CITY]-based [ETHNIC GROUP] institutions, such as the [CITY] Chamber of Commerce and [BUILDING], which had been the de facto [REBEL GROUP] office. The government renewed a military closure order for these and other institutions on the grounds they violated [AGREEMENT] by conducting political activities or otherwise operating on behalf of [REBEL GROUP] in [CITY]. The government likewise continued to shut down [ETHNIC GROUP] institutions and cultural events in [CITY] due to [REBEL GROUP] participation or support. According to [MEDIA OUTLET], the minister of Public Security approved [POPULATION FIGURE] such orders during the period.

