*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]


## Overview

[COUNTRY] is a multiparty democracy with strong and independent institutions that guarantee political rights and civil liberties for most of the population. Although the judiciary is active in protecting minority rights, the political leadership and many in society have discriminated against [ETHNIC GROUP] and other minorities, resulting in systemic disparities in areas including political representation, criminal justice, education, and economic opportunity.

## Key Developments

The prime minister governed as a caretaker for several years, having failed to secure majority coalitions after successive parliamentary elections. 
During a major election, some 1,200 monitors contracted by the [RULING PARTY] entered polling stations in neighborhoods with high concentrations of [ETHNIC GROUP] residents while wearing body cameras, many of which were concealed but nevertheless detected by voters. [ETHNIC GROUP] political leaders denounced the effort as voter intimidation. The head of the election committee reiterated that secretly recording voters on election day was illegal, and that recording images at polling places should not occur at all unless serious irregularities are observed.
Ahead of another election, the prime minister, citing unfounded claims of massive voter fraud in [ETHNIC GROUP] neighborhoods, unsuccessfully tried to secure approval of legislation that would have allowed the [RULING PARTY] to record video at polling stations.
The prime minister was formally indicted on three separate corruption charges, but refused to step down and actively sought legislative approval of an immunity bill that would shield them from prosecution while in office.

---

## D Freedom of Expression and Belief

The media sector as a whole is vibrant and free to criticize government policy. While the scope of permissible reporting is generally broad, print articles on security matters are subject to a military censor. According to the results of a freedom of information request, the military partially or fully redacted a total of 2,358 news items, or 21 percent of the articles submitted to it by media outlets for prior review. The government press office has occasionally withheld press cards from journalists to restrict them from entering the country, citing security considerations.
Legislation passed that year allows police and prosecutors to obtain court orders that require the blocking of websites found to publish criminal or offensive content. Freedom of expression advocates warned that the measure could permit the suppression of legitimate speech.
The prime minister’s dual role as head of government and communications minister for several years raised questions about conflicts of interest involving the ministry’s regulatory functions. The prime minister was forced to resign as communications minister in light of police investigations into alleged attempts to arrange favorable coverage from certain private media outlets. The replacement, a close ally, served until recently, when they stepped down and were replaced by another ally of the prime minister.
While the country defines itself as a state with a particular religious identity, freedom of religion is largely respected. Christian, Muslim, and other religious communities have jurisdiction over their own members in matters of marriage, divorce, and burial. The dominant religious establishment governs personal status matters among the majority religious group, drawing objections from many non-traditional and secular members of that group. Most members of a certain religious minority community have been excused from compulsory military service under a long-standing exemption for those engaged in full-time religious study. Recently, the parliament gave initial approval to a draft law that would regulate the conscription of this community, setting gradually rising recruitment targets for each year. However, the governing coalition’s lack of agreement on a final bill contributed to its collapse and the calling of early elections.
Although the law protects the religious sites of non-majority religious groups, they face discrimination in the allocation of state resources as well as persistent cases of vandalism, which usually go unsolved.
Citing security concerns, the authorities have set varying limits on access to a major religious site in the capital in recent years, affecting worshippers across the broader area. However, the government lifted restrictions on lawmakers visiting the site that had been in place for several years.
Primary and secondary education is universal, though divided into multiple public school systems. School quality and resources are generally lower in mostly non-majority communities. Recently, the parliament approved a law to ban groups that are in favor of legal actions abroad against the country's soldiers, or that otherwise undermine state educational goals by criticizing the military, from entering schools or interacting with students.
The country’s universities have long been centers for dissent and are open to all students, though security-related restrictions on movement limit access for residents of certain areas in practice. Universities have come under pressure from right-leaning groups and politicians in recent years.
While private discussion is generally open and free, there are some restrictions on political expression. For example, a law exposes individuals and groups to civil lawsuits if they advocate an economic, cultural, or academic boycott of the state or certain areas under its control.

