*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]


## Overview

Following a peaceful revolution, [COUNTRY] began holding multiparty elections and established itself as an electoral democracy. Political rights and civil liberties have been firmly institutionalized, though the two dominant parties continue to rely on patronage networks, and widespread corruption increasingly hampers further development.

## Key Developments

In a major legislative move, the parliament passed legislation allowing the national security council to recommend the dismissal of the anticorruption agency’s chief, top prosecutors, and judges. The anticorruption chief, supreme court chief justice, and prosecutor general were dismissed by the president later that month, while another 17 judges were dismissed a few months later.
Later that year, [COUNTRY] adopted constitutional amendments that will strengthen the power of the prime minister; the amendments will also limit future presidents to a single six-year term. 
Earlier, the parliamentary speaker was ousted by the legislature. The speaker was implicated in the sale of state offices for several years prior, and was accused by the prime minister of engineering an unsuccessful no-confidence vote against him in recent years.

---

## C Functioning of Government

Freely elected representatives are duly seated and generally able to craft government policy without improper interference. However, corporations, aided by opaque party finance procedures, have also been able to influence policymaking.
Corruption is endemic in [COUNTRY] and is widely perceived to have worsened in recent years, particularly with respect to state involvement in the mining sector. Anticorruption laws are vaguely written and infrequently enforced. Investigations into corruption allegations are often dropped by prosecutors before reaching definitive conclusions. The [NGO] has been criticized as ineffective in pursuing cases, most prominently one in which the parliament speaker allegedly sought to sell state offices to bolster [RULING PARTY] finances. 
Evidence emerged that leading political figures and state officials siphoned funds from a government program meant to support small and medium-sized enterprises. A faction within the [RULING PARTY] attempted to oust the government with an unsuccessful no-confidence vote. The prime minister denounced the vote as an attempt by the former parliament speaker and his allies to protect their own corrupt interests, and the former speaker was removed as speaker. His successor assumed the post.
The [NGO]’s independence was weakened when emergency legislation allowed the [security forces] leadership, made up of the president, prime minister, and parliament speaker, to recommend the chief’s dismissal before the end of their term. Later, the president dismissed the chief on the recommendation; the prosecutor general, who previously called for prosecutions against lawmakers implicated in corruption, was also dismissed.
