## [COUNTRY]


## Overview

While [COUNTRY] holds regular elections, their credibility has deteriorated over time, and the country has been ruled by the same party and the president for decades. The ruling party, [RULING PARTY], retains power through the manipulation of state resources, intimidation by the security forces, and politicized prosecutions of opposition leaders. [COUNTRY]'s civil society and independent media sectors suffer from legal and extralegal harassment and state violence.

## Key Developments

Police repeatedly used force to disrupt rallies, protests, and other events organized by the political opposition, in some cases arresting opposition leaders.
The media regulator ordered the suspension of 39 journalists at 13 outlets, though the move was later blocked by a court, and issued a directive requiring social media accounts with large followings to register and submit to official monitoring.
A prominent academic was sentenced to 18 months in prison under legislation for posts that were critical of the president.
The interior minister called on thousands of [NGO]s to cease their activities after they failed to pass a reregistration process.

---

## C Functioning of Government

Power is concentrated in the hands of the [RULING PARTY] leadership, the security forces, and especially the president, who retains office through deeply flawed electoral processes. Lawmakers have little practical ability to influence legislation in which the government has a particular interest, though there is more consultation on ordinary policy matters. The executive has secured passage of key legislation through inducement, harassment, and intimidation of the legislative branch. For example, several [OPPOSITION PARTY] lawmakers were assaulted and forcibly removed from the parliament by plainclothes military officers during the reading of legislation that removed the presidential age limit.
Corruption is a serious problem. There are laws and institutions designed to combat official malfeasance, including legislation and institutions designed to combat corruption, and instances of alleged graft have led to investigations and intense media attention. However, the system has not been effective at addressing corruption in a sustained manner, and top government officials are rarely prosecuted in practice.
Many government departments deny requests for information under the [COUNTRY]'s access to information laws. Other laws related to national security and confidentiality also impede open access to information in practice. Public procurement decisions are generally opaque.

